# Discover roots and publish stable service-owned aliases

## Context

Design: `.collab/design/2026-10-19-c2c-t3-external-connector.md`.
Pending planned work. During execution follow bl-fix-many: read the full ticket, verify blockers, commit the claim, work in an isolated worktree, review/fix, run the c2c gates on build boxes, merge and close. No T3 source changes. Tests use x-p35/x-tuf, never the workstation's full suite. Actual just bi and service installation wait for the current candidate's successful P9.M2.E1.T001 buildbox gate.

## Requirements

- Own the initial C2c_t3_service discovery and alias lifecycle, plus narrow broker/CLI resolver wiring. Use the client/state interfaces from T002/T003.
- Enumerate all projects and every thread page with includeSubagents false, then enforce parentThreadId null and not deleted/archived/settled. Idle/failed open roots are included; forks with parents are excluded.
- Expose permission-ineligible full-access roots as unroutable without upgrading the token. Partial scans and API outages cannot retire missing rows. Completed offset pages are also mutable: point-read every missing bound thread before retirement, requiring affirmative retirement or definitive same-environment not-found. Failed reads keep state with degraded health.
- Persist before advertising t3-hash aliases in the shared c2c broker with service PID/start-time ownership. Native hooks must neither adopt nor accidentally drain these service-owned inboxes.
- Support local t3.thread-id lookup only when unambiguous and never shadow a real alias. Remote addressing is existing alias@host_id.
- Graceful retirement retains alias tombstone and pending/dedupe state. Reopening the same thread may reuse only its own alias.

## Acceptance Criteria

- Fixture tests cover roots versus forks/subagents, title change, duplicate names, all pages/projects, incomplete scan, a last-page thread moving into the first page during enumeration, permission limits, retirement and reopen. None of these list races may retire a live binding.
- c2c list/find and c2c t3 list distinguish routable, stale, held and retired aliases; titles do not leak into relay routing names.
- No T3/provider configuration change or inherited native identity is needed. Ownership guards do not disable unrelated clients.
- Two roots in the same directory remain independently routable across service restarts.

## Lane

Serial shared-file integration lane after T002/T003. T005 follows in the same lane. Coordinate any broker/relay ownership helpers with T006.
